                <pb facs="0426" n="308" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg308"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.113/1155</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-12-05"><hi rendition="#italic">December 5,
                        1928</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received January 7, 1929.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1787</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Legation’s despatch
                    No. 1437, of March 19, 1928,<anchor n="77" xml:id="d269-f1"/> and to previous
                    communications regarding the Arms Embargo Agreement of 1919, I have the honor to
                    state that the French Minister, shortly after his recent return from leave of
                    absence in France, called a meeting of the representatives of the Powers
                    signatory to the Agreement, to consider, under existing circumstances, the
                    desirability of maintaining, modifying, or doing away with it. The meeting, a
                    translation of the Minutes of which is enclosed,<anchor n="77" xml:id="d269-f2"
                    /> was held on November 23rd. It will be observed that it was decided to
                    maintain the <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> in respect to the Arms
                    Embargo.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. V. A.
                    MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
